  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 252 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nOn Wednesday, 500 mb level ensemble guidance forecast \nnorthwesterly flow over the area as we are expected to be in \nbetween a deep trough in the southeast and a weak ridge to the \nover the middle Rockies starts to move to the east. After this\non Friday, it is expected for the ridge move into Minnesota\nwhile a new trough in the southwest forms and digs to the south.\nWhile this would put us into northerly flow, there are hints\nthat northwesterly flow to continue as variability to where the\ntrough be located in the southwest as spread in the GEFS\nensemble starts to grow. However, this spread is small and there\nis medium to high confidence (55-75%) that this will likely not\nbe favored occur. Towards the surface, a broad high pressure\nsystem to east would likely be present and would allow calm \nwinds to be fed in from the south bringing highs back into the\n40s. Clouds are forecast to be scattered.\n\nOn Friday and Saturday, we are expected to return back to\nnorthwesterly flow again as the area would be between two large\nand amplifying features. In the West, a ridge is forecast to\nform off of the Pacific Coast and quickly slide into the\nRockies. In the east, a trough system forms north of the old\nridge system and quickly amplifies to the south into the\nmidwest. At the 850 mb layer, a low level jet stream is forecast\nto dip down to the surface with the 500 mb trough. This would\nallow winds at the surface to pick up from the northwest up to\n25 mph on Friday over the Tri-State region. Temperatures looks\nto continue to trend upward with highs potentially reaching into\nthe 50s and lows in the 30s. Clouds are expected to remain\nscattered.",
